Janis Papanagnou wrote:

 > With a standardized C++ language we're in a much
 > better starting position than Rogue/Hack/Nethack
 > had been at former times with C.

Modify that to say "with a standardized OOPL
language" and I agree with you. I don't see C++ as
an especially attractive choice, and my programming
in C++ began way back in 1992. The language is an
ugly set of compromises.

 > Machine depending details (e.g. size of data
 > types) and platform specific GUI aspects would
 > have to be considered anyway, similar to the
 > current code base,

For better standardized languages than C++, those
aspects are no longer part of the human workload,
the compiler takes care of them in the sense that
there is only one choice, no matter where the
software runs.

[It's frankly astonishing that Stroustrup didn't get
rid of this mess of non-standardized data sizes in
the transition from C to C++.]

There are also other useful standards available,
like host-neutral data transmission formats that
turn the byte orders around if necessary to have
data streams all transmitted identically across
a wire no matter the byte orders of the machines on
each end.

That would help support an NAO type interface (if
the software is modularized into communicating
sequential processes, one doing the display, one
accepting keystrokes and mouse input, and one
running the game, so that playing tiles mode across
a server interface works).

 > but all the OO languages can support a design for
 > significantly cleaner interfaces (low level and
 > OS).

Yes.

As noted elsewhere, even for languages that don't
have a native curses/ncurses interface, one will be
available as an add on library.

Personally, though, I think that NetHack-Next(1)
should work entirely in tiles mode, and the ASCII
character set be just another alternate set of tiles
for those who prefer an ASCII display. [That doesn't
eliminate that ncurses-compatible calls could drive
that tiled display.]

For those who like to use macros pasted in as text,
that should either be an added functionality (a
new macro facility) or else an added interface,
since what is being entered as macros isn't
"display" emulations anyway, it is a keystroke
emulation, and should be possible to paste into the
keyboard interface.

Similarly, no matter which set of tiles is in use,
ASCII or otherwise, it should be possible to do a
screen capture into plain ASCII text (discarding
character colors) for use here in rgrn, as well as
to do a screen capture of any set of tiles in use
as a layer into a *.PNG image.

